  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through next Thursday)\nIssued at 200 PM CDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nMost CAMs suggest the cold front pushing through the Panhandles on \nSunday morning and early afternoon. While this will likely bring \ncooler temperatures across the entirety of the Panhandles, it also \nmeans that by the time peak daytime heating is reached and \nconvergence along the cold front initiates thunderstorms, the front \nwill be southeast of the Panhandles, therefore limiting rain\npotential. By Monday, the upper- level pattern from the weekend \nflattening out and propagating east and a new upper-level trough \nis moving into southern California. A shortwave trough in zonal \nflow is responsible for possible precipitation chances across the \nsouthern Panhandles on Monday afternoon/evening, but the better \nchance for precipitation arrives Tuesday. As the trough \npropagates further east, it places the Panhandles in southwesterly\nupper- level flow, inviting monsoonal moisture from the Pacific \nand return moisture from the gulf due to high pressure over \nsoutheast Texas. This pattern also promotes lee-side surface low \ndevelopment, which could produce multiple days of active weather \nfrom Tuesday onward, depending on the speed of the new trough\ndevelopment moving in from the west.\n\nFerguson",
